2. Glossary of Key Terms
This section provides precise, research-backed definitions for every domain-specific term referenced throughout the document. Mastery of this terminology is essential for correct system design and strategy validation.

  2.1 Market Data & Microstructure Terms
OHLCV	Open-High-Low-Close-Volume. The five canonical fields of a price bar or candlestick. Open is the first trade price; High/Low are the intra-period extremes; Close is the final trade price; Volume is the total number of units transacted. OHLCV bars are the atomic unit of the feature engineering pipeline.
Tick Data	The raw, timestamped record of every individual trade or quote update in a market. Tick data preserves intra-bar dynamics lost in aggregated bars, enabling microstructure analysis.
Order Book	The real-time electronic list of all outstanding buy (bid) and sell (ask) limit orders at each price level. Depth-of-book data reveals supply/demand imbalances invisible in OHLCV data.
Bid-Ask Spread	The difference between the lowest ask price and the highest bid price at any moment. It represents the implicit transaction cost and a key microstructure signal.
Order Book Imbalance (OBI)	A normalized ratio comparing total bid volume to total ask volume within N price levels: OBI = (BidVol − AskVol) / (BidVol + AskVol). Values near +1 signal buying pressure; near −1 signal selling pressure.
Market Impact	The adverse price movement caused by executing a large order. Accurate market impact models are critical for realistic backtesting of strategies exceeding small notional sizes.
Slippage	The difference between the expected execution price and the actual fill price, arising from latency, market impact, and partial fills. The backtesting engine models slippage explicitly.
VWAP	Volume-Weighted Average Price. The ratio of cumulative dollar value traded to cumulative volume over a period. Often used as an execution benchmark.
TWAP	Time-Weighted Average Price. The arithmetic mean of prices sampled at regular time intervals. Simpler than VWAP and used in execution algorithms that slice orders over time.

  2.2 Technical Analysis Indicators
SMA / EMA	Simple Moving Average / Exponential Moving Average. SMA averages closing prices over N periods with equal weights. EMA applies geometrically declining weights (factor α = 2/(N+1)), making it more responsive to recent data.
RSI	Relative Strength Index. A momentum oscillator in [0,100] computed as RSI = 100 − 100/(1 + RS), where RS = average gain / average loss over the lookback window. Values above 70 signal overbought conditions; below 30 signal oversold.
MACD	Moving Average Convergence Divergence. The difference between the 12-period and 26-period EMA. A 9-period EMA of MACD forms the signal line. Crossovers and divergences generate trade signals.
Bollinger Bands	A volatility envelope defined by a central SMA ± k standard deviations (typically k=2). Band width is a proxy for realized volatility; price touching the upper/lower band can signal mean-reversion entries.
ATR	Average True Range. Measures volatility as the moving average of max(High−Low, |High−Prev Close|, |Low−Prev Close|). Used for position sizing and stop-loss placement.
Stochastic Oscillator	Compares the closing price to its price range over N periods: %K = (Close − Lowest Low) / (Highest High − Lowest Low) × 100. Signals overbought/oversold conditions.
ADX	Average Directional Index. Measures trend strength (0–100) without regard to direction. Readings above 25 indicate a trending regime; below 20 indicate ranging conditions.
OBV	On-Balance Volume. Cumulative volume indicator: adds volume on up-days and subtracts on down-days. Divergence between OBV and price can precede trend reversals.

  2.3 Machine Learning & Statistical Terms
Ensemble Method	A meta-algorithm that combines predictions from multiple base learners to reduce variance, bias, or both. Bagging (e.g., Random Forest) reduces variance; Boosting (e.g., Gradient Boosting) reduces bias iteratively.
Random Forest	An ensemble of decision trees trained on bootstrap samples of the data with random feature subsets at each split. The final prediction averages all tree outputs, dramatically reducing overfitting relative to a single tree.
Gradient Boosting	A sequential ensemble where each new tree models the residual errors of the previous ensemble. XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost are industry-standard implementations offering regularization, GPU acceleration, and native categorical support.
LSTM	Long Short-Term Memory. A recurrent neural network variant with gated memory cells (input, forget, output gates) that can capture long-range temporal dependencies in sequential data such as price series.
Transformer / Attention	A neural architecture that replaces recurrence with self-attention mechanisms, allowing every time step to directly attend to every other step. State-of-the-art for sequence modeling when combined with positional encoding.
Feature Importance	A measure of each input feature's contribution to model predictions. Tree-based models compute it via impurity reduction; permutation importance is model-agnostic.
SHAP Values	SHapley Additive exPlanations. A game-theory-based framework assigning each feature a contribution score for each individual prediction, enabling local and global model interpretability.
Hyperparameter Tuning	The process of searching for the model configuration (e.g., tree depth, learning rate) that maximizes out-of-sample performance. Techniques include grid search, random search, and Bayesian optimization.
Regularization	Techniques (L1 / Lasso, L2 / Ridge, Dropout) that penalize model complexity to reduce overfitting by constraining parameter magnitudes or randomly zeroing activations during training.
Class Imbalance	A dataset condition where one outcome (e.g., profitable trade) is far rarer than another. Addressed via oversampling (SMOTE), undersampling, cost-sensitive learning, or threshold tuning.

  2.4 Backtesting & Performance Terms
Backtesting	Simulating a trading strategy on historical data to estimate how it would have performed in the past. Rigorous backtesting controls for survivorship bias, look-ahead bias, and transaction costs.
Walk-Forward Validation	A rolling evaluation framework: train on window [t₀, t₁], test on [t₁, t₂], slide the window forward, repeat. This mimics live trading conditions and is the gold standard for out-of-sample validation.
Look-Ahead Bias	The erroneous use of future data in a backtest — e.g., using tomorrow's closing price to generate today's signal. Eliminates all predictive credibility of the backtest.
Survivorship Bias	The distortion caused by only including assets that survived to the present in a historical dataset. Strategies tested only on surviving assets appear artificially better.
Sharpe Ratio	Excess return per unit of total risk: SR = (R_p − R_f) / σ_p, where R_p is portfolio return, R_f is the risk-free rate, and σ_p is annualized standard deviation of returns. Higher is better; >1 is acceptable, >2 is strong.
Sortino Ratio	A variant of the Sharpe Ratio that penalizes only downside volatility (semi-deviation), not upside volatility. More relevant for strategies with asymmetric return distributions.
Maximum Drawdown (MDD)	The largest peak-to-trough decline in portfolio value over the measurement period: MDD = (Trough − Peak) / Peak. Measures the worst-case loss an investor would have experienced.
Calmar Ratio	Annualized return divided by Maximum Drawdown. Balances return against worst-case loss: Calmar = CAGR / |MDD|.
Value at Risk (VaR)	A probabilistic risk metric: VaR(α) = the loss not exceeded with probability α over a given horizon. E.g., 1-day 95% VaR = maximum loss on 95% of trading days.
Expected Shortfall (CVaR)	The expected loss conditional on exceeding VaR: CVaR = E[Loss | Loss > VaR]. More coherent than VaR because it captures tail risk; preferred by regulators post-2008.
Alpha / Beta	Alpha = excess return above a benchmark attributable to skill; Beta = sensitivity of portfolio returns to benchmark returns. Alpha > 0 with Beta < 1 is the ideal profile.

  2.5 Portfolio & Risk Management Terms
Position Sizing	The process of determining the notional or share quantity for each trade. Kelly Criterion, fixed fractional, and volatility-targeting are the primary frameworks.
Kelly Criterion	An optimal position sizing formula: f* = (bp − q) / b, where b is the odds ratio, p is win probability, and q = 1 − p. Full Kelly maximizes long-run geometric growth but produces extreme drawdowns; practitioners use fractional Kelly (e.g., 0.25×).
Leverage	The ratio of total exposure to net asset value. A leverage of 2× means for every $1 of capital, $2 of positions are held. Leverage amplifies both gains and losses.
Drawdown Control	Mechanisms that reduce or halt trading when losses exceed thresholds — e.g., cutting leverage in half when MDD exceeds 10%.
Correlation	The statistical co-movement between two return series (Pearson's ρ ∈ [−1, +1]). High positive correlation between holdings reduces diversification benefit.
Beta Hedging	Shorting a market index (or futures) proportionally to the portfolio's beta to neutralize systematic market exposure, isolating alpha.
                                           
