    3.2 Core Architectural Principles
•	Modularity: Each of the five layers (ingestion, features, ML, execution, analytics) is independently deployable. Module boundaries are defined by Pydantic data schemas and Kafka topics, not shared in-process state.
•	Idempotency: Every data transformation is deterministic and re-runnable. Re-processing historical data produces identical results, enabling reproducible research and audit trails.
•	Event-Driven Communication: Apache Kafka acts as the backbone message bus. Producers emit events (raw ticks, computed features, ML predictions, order fills); consumers react asynchronously, decoupling throughput constraints.
•	Separation of Concerns: Strategy logic is strictly isolated from execution logic. A strategy emits a signal (direction, confidence, suggested size); the execution layer independently decides how and when to act, applying real-time risk checks.
•	Observability by Design: Every service emits structured logs, metrics (Prometheus), and distributed traces (OpenTelemetry) from inception. Debugging a production issue must be possible without modifying code.

    3.3 Data Flow Summary
1.	External market data (REST + WebSocket) arrives at the Ingestion Service, which validates, deduplicates, and emits raw events to Kafka topic raw-market-data.
2.	The Feature Engineering Service consumes raw events, computes all technical indicators and microstructure signals, and publishes enriched feature vectors to Kafka topic engineered-features.
3.	The Model Serving Service consumes feature vectors, runs inference through pre-trained ensemble and LSTM models, and publishes prediction events (signal direction + probability) to Kafka topic ml-predictions.
4.	The Strategy Engine consumes both rule-based signals and ML predictions, applies the signal combination logic, and emits Order Proposals to Kafka topic order-proposals.
5.	The Risk Gate validates each proposal against real-time portfolio constraints (leverage, VaR, concentration limits) and either approves or rejects it.
6.	The Order Management System (OMS) routes approved orders to the broker API, tracks fill status, and emits Fill Events back into Kafka.
7.	The Portfolio Manager consumes Fill Events to maintain real-time position, P&L, and risk metrics, serving them to the Analytics Dashboard via a time-series database.
 
4. Feature Engineering Pipeline — Deep Dive
The feature engineering pipeline is the critical first transformation stage. Its quality directly determines the upper bound of model performance: no ML algorithm can reliably extract signal from poorly constructed or contaminated features.

  4.1 Data Ingestion & Quality Controls

                                                                   4.1.1 Data Sources
•	Primary OHLCV: Polygon.io, Alpha Vantage, or Refinitiv (minute-to-daily bars, decades of history).
•	Real-Time WebSocket: Direct exchange feeds (Binance, Coinbase, NYSE via FIX protocol) or broker-provided streams.
•	Fundamental Data: Earnings, dividends, splits via SEC EDGAR API or vendor feeds (used for event-risk filters).
•	Alternative Data: Sentiment scores from news/social media APIs (e.g., RavenPack, Quandl) for regime context features.

                                                                   4.1.2 Data Quality Checklist
•	Gap detection: flag missing bars using expected time grid comparison (NYSE session: 09:30–16:00 ET).
•	Outlier filtering: z-score |z| > 5 on returns triggers human review; Winsorize at 3σ for automated pipelines.
•	Split/dividend adjustment: apply back-adjusted prices using vendor-provided adjustment factors before any feature computation.
•	Timestamp normalization: convert all timestamps to UTC; handle daylight saving transitions explicitly.
•	Deduplication: hash-based deduplication on (symbol, timestamp, source) tuples.

  4.2 Momentum Indicators — Mathematical Definitions
All indicators are computed on adjusted close prices unless stated otherwise. Normalization (zero-mean, unit-variance) is applied after computation, not before, to preserve the indicator's economic meaning.
EMA(t, N) = α × Close(t) + (1−α) × EMA(t−1, N),   α = 2/(N+1)
RSI(N) = 100 − 100 / (1 + AvgGain(N) / AvgLoss(N))
MACD = EMA(12) − EMA(26);   Signal = EMA(9, MACD);   Histogram = MACD − Signal
ROC(N) = (Close(t) − Close(t−N)) / Close(t−N) × 100

   4.3 Volatility Indicators
ATR(N) = SMA(N, TrueRange),   TrueRange = max(H−L, |H−C_prev|, |L−C_prev|)
HV(N) = σ(log returns, N) × √252        [annualized]
BB_width = (BB_upper − BB_lower) / BB_mid × 100
Volatility features serve two purposes: as direct predictive signals (volatility forecasting) and as position-sizing inputs (the denominator in volatility-targeted sizing).

   4.4 Market Microstructure Features

                                                                   4.4.1 Order Book Imbalance
OBI(L) = (ΣBid_vol(1..L) − ΣAsk_vol(1..L)) / (ΣBid_vol(1..L) + ΣAsk_vol(1..L))
Typical levels L ∈ {1, 5, 10}.  OBI ∈ [−1, +1]

                                                                   4.4.2 Trade Flow Imbalance
Classifies each trade as buyer- or seller-initiated using the Lee-Ready algorithm (trade price vs. prevailing midpoint). Net buy volume fraction over rolling windows (1s, 10s, 60s) produces short-horizon directional features with predictive power in the 0–15 minute horizon.

                                                                   4.4.3 Tick-Level Volatility
Realized variance estimated from high-frequency returns: RV(M) = Σ r²_i over M intraday intervals. Subsampled and bias-corrected using the two-scales estimator (Zhang, Mykland & Aït-Sahalia, 2005) to remove market microstructure noise.

   4.5 Feature Normalization & Encoding Strategy
Feature Type	Normalization Method	Rationale
Price-based (SMA, EMA)	Percentage deviation from rolling mean	Removes non-stationarity
Oscillators (RSI, Stoch)	Already bounded [0,100] — rescale to [−1,+1]	Symmetry for ML models
Volume features	Log transform, then z-score	Removes right-skew
Microstructure (OBI, TFI)	Min-max [−1,+1] per rolling window	Preserves sign information
Returns	Winsorize ±3σ, then z-score	Reduces outlier influence


    4.6 Feature Store Design
All computed features are written to a Feature Store — a versioned key-value store backed by Redis (hot, <1ms latency) and Parquet files on object storage (cold, full history). This separation enables:
•	Point-in-time correct retrieval: queries specify a 'as-of' timestamp, preventing look-ahead during both backtesting and live trading.
•	Feature versioning: each feature set is tagged with a schema hash; model training logs the exact feature version used.
•	Reuse: multiple strategies and models consume features without re-computing them.
 
5. Machine Learning & Predictive Modeling
                                                                   
The ML layer translates engineered features into probabilistic trading signals. The design philosophy prioritizes robustness over complexity: a well-calibrated gradient boosting model with proper cross-validation consistently outperforms a sophisticated neural network that is overfit.

    5.1 Problem Formulation
Trading signal generation is cast as a multi-class classification problem with three target classes: UP (+1), FLAT (0), DOWN (−1). The label for each bar is computed from the forward return over horizon H:
Label(t) = sign(Close(t+H) − Close(t)) filtered by |return| > threshold θ
Unlabeled (FLAT) when |return| ≤ θ to reduce noise in the training signal
Typical: H = 5 bars (5-min chart ⟹ 25-min forward return), θ = 0.05%
This formulation avoids regression on raw returns (which are extremely noisy) and instead focuses the model on high-conviction directional moves.

    5.2 Ensemble Methods — Architecture

                                                                   5.2.1 Random Forest
•	Base learners: decision trees with max_depth ∈ {6, 8, 10}, tuned via cross-validation.
•	Bagging: each tree trained on 70% bootstrap sample with √(n_features) features per split.
•	Voting: soft voting (probability averaging) across 500 trees.
•	Advantage: naturally outputs well-calibrated probabilities; highly interpretable via feature importance.

                                                                   5.2.2 Gradient Boosting (XGBoost / LightGBM)
•	Sequential residual fitting with η (learning rate) ∈ {0.01, 0.05, 0.1} and early stopping on validation log-loss.
•	Regularization: L1 (alpha) and L2 (lambda) penalties on leaf weights; minimum child weight constraint prevents over-specific splits.
•	LightGBM preference: leaf-wise tree growth (vs. level-wise in XGBoost) produces deeper, more accurate trees on financial tabular data.
•	GPU acceleration available via LightGBM device='gpu' for training sets exceeding 10M rows.

                                                                   5.2.3 Meta-Learner (Stacking)
