5.3.1 Input Preparation
•	Sequence length: 60 time steps (bars), representing one trading hour on a 1-minute chart.
•	Feature dimensions: 48 features per step (technical indicators + microstructure).
•	Batch normalization applied per feature across the training set; stored statistics used at inference.
5.3.2 Network Architecture
Layer	Type	Parameters	Notes
1	LSTM	units=128, return_sequences=True	First temporal encoding layer
2	Dropout	rate=0.3	Prevents co-adaptation
3	LSTM	units=64, return_sequences=False	Compressed temporal representation
4	Dropout	rate=0.2	
5	Dense	units=32, activation='relu'	Non-linear feature mixing
6	Dense	units=3, activation='softmax'	Output: P(UP), P(FLAT), P(DOWN)

5.3.3 Training Protocol
•	Optimizer: Adam with learning rate 1e-3, β₁=0.9, β₂=0.999.
•	Loss: Categorical cross-entropy with class weights inversely proportional to class frequency.
•	Early stopping: monitor validation loss, patience=15 epochs, restore best weights.
•	Gradient clipping: max_norm=1.0 to stabilize training on volatile financial data.
5.4 Signal Calibration
Raw model output probabilities are often poorly calibrated — a model reporting 70% probability may not win 70% of the time. Platt Scaling (logistic regression on model scores) and Isotonic Regression are applied post-training and validated via reliability diagrams and the Expected Calibration Error (ECE) metric. Calibrated probabilities directly feed the Kelly position sizing formula.
5.5 Model Serving & Inference Latency
•	Models are serialized to ONNX format for runtime-agnostic inference with sub-millisecond latency.
•	ONNX Runtime with TensorRT backend achieves <0.5ms per inference on GPU for the LSTM model.
•	Ensemble inference is parallelized across CPU cores via ThreadPoolExecutor.
•	Feature computation + inference target: end-to-end <10ms for live trading use cases.
 
6. Backtesting Engine — Architecture & Anti-Bias Controls
The backtesting engine is the quality gate between strategy conception and capital deployment. Its design must rigorously eliminate all known sources of simulation bias while accurately modeling real-world execution frictions.
6.1 Walk-Forward Validation Framework
Walk-Forward Optimization (WFO) divides the historical dataset into overlapping in-sample (IS) and out-of-sample (OOS) windows:
For window w in {1, …, W}:
  IS(w)  = [t_0 + (w−1)×step,  t_0 + (w−1)×step + IS_length]
  OOS(w) = [IS(w).end,          IS(w).end + OOS_length]
  Train model on IS(w) → Evaluate on OOS(w) → Record OOS metrics
Final assessment = aggregate of all OOS(w) performance
•	Typical ratio: IS = 24 months, OOS = 6 months, step = 3 months ⟹ rolling 3-month forward tests.
•	Anchored vs. sliding: Anchored WFO keeps IS start fixed (accumulating data); sliding WFO uses fixed-length IS window (prevents stale data dominance).
6.2 Bias Prevention Mechanisms
Bias Type	Risk	Mitigation
Look-ahead bias	Using future data in current signals	Strict timeline enforcement; all features tagged with computation timestamp
Survivorship bias	Testing only on assets that exist today	Use point-in-time universe database; include delisted securities
Overfitting / curve-fitting	Strategy tuned to historical noise	Walk-forward validation; out-of-sample testing; parameter sensitivity analysis
Data-snooping bias	Testing many strategies on same data	Track 'degrees of freedom' used; apply Bonferroni correction to p-values
Transaction cost underestimation	Ignoring real execution frictions	Model bid-ask spread, market impact, and borrow costs explicitly
Regime bias	Testing only in favorable conditions	Stress-test across bull, bear, and sideways sub-periods

6.3 Transaction Cost Modeling
Realistic transaction cost modeling is the single most common source of backtesting optimism. The system implements three cost layers:
•	Spread cost: Half the prevailing bid-ask spread at time of execution, sourced from historical quote data where available or estimated from ATR-based proxy models.
•	Market impact: A square-root market impact model: MI = η × σ × √(Q/ADV), where Q is order quantity, ADV is average daily volume, σ is daily volatility, and η is a calibration constant (typically 0.1–0.5 for equities).
•	Borrow cost: For short positions, applies the stock borrow rate (from historical securities lending data or proxy rates by market cap and sector).
6.4 Market Regime Classification
The backtesting engine labels every bar with its market regime using a Hidden Markov Model (HMM) fitted to volatility and trend features. Three regimes are distinguished:
Regime	Characteristics	Strategy Behavior
Bull Trending	Positive trend, low-medium vol, high ADX	Momentum strategies outperform; reduce mean-reversion exposure
Bear Trending	Negative trend, elevated vol, high ADX	Short momentum; increase tail-risk hedges
Sideways / Ranging	Low trend, low ADX, oscillating price	Mean-reversion strategies outperform; reduce momentum exposure
High Volatility Shock	VIX spike, fat-tailed returns, low liquidity	Reduce all positions; activate volatility-targeting sizing

6.5 Performance Report Structure
