14. A/B Testing & Continuous Research Framework
The platform is designed to continuously evaluate new strategies, models, and features against the live baseline through a rigorous statistical A/B testing framework.
14.1 Experiment Design
•	Traffic splitting: Capital allocation is split between control (current best strategy) and treatment (new strategy). Default split: 90%/10% during initial evaluation.
•	Duration: Minimum 63 trading days (3 calendar months) to achieve statistical significance at α=0.05 for typical financial return distributions.
•	Primary metric: Risk-adjusted return (Sharpe Ratio) over the test period.
•	Secondary metrics: Win rate, average holding time, MDD, VaR, and transaction costs per trade.
•	Sequential testing: Use O'Brien-Fleming alpha spending function to allow early stopping when treatment superiority is overwhelming.
14.2 Hypothesis Testing Framework
Null Hypothesis H₀: Sharpe(treatment) ≤ Sharpe(control)
Alternative  H₁: Sharpe(treatment) > Sharpe(control)

Test Statistic: Two-sample t-test on rolling 21-day Sharpe estimates
Significance level α = 0.05, Power 1−β = 0.80
Minimum detectable effect: ΔΔSR = 0.15 (15% relative improvement)

Multiple testing correction: Benjamini-Hochberg FDR at q = 0.10
for simultaneous evaluation of multiple treatment arms
14.3 Research Workflow
97.	Researcher proposes hypothesis in Jupyter notebook; documents in experiment registry (Notion + MLflow).
98.	Backtest on holdout period (never used in model training); must pass minimum Sharpe and MDD criteria.
99.	Code review and merge to feature branch after peer review.
100.	Deploy to paper trading with 5% capital allocation for 2 weeks to validate signal latency and execution quality.
101.	Promote to A/B test with 10% allocation upon paper trading sign-off.
102.	Statistical significance test after 63 days; promote to 50% then 100% if H₀ rejected.
103.	Retire control strategy; update production model registry.
 
